welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Cheryl das a senior lecturer in  international affairs and economics at  Yale University professor das is  research interest center around  household decision-making in rural  households especially in Africa in  particular she is interested in how  social and economic changes affect the  dynamics of decision-making within  households much of Professor doses  current work focuses on issues around  women's access to assets including land  today we talk with her about land  ownership and land tenure reform as it  affects women in Africa  welcome professor das thank you let's  begin with how you got involved in  researching the dynamics of households  in rural households especially in Africa  what led you to it I had spent some time  living and working in Africa I worked in  Liberia working with a women's training  program and got very interested in  trying to understand what was happening  with women and then when I started  graduate school in applied economics it  turns out that most of the models of  households treated households as though  they were individuals and so thought  about household decision-making as  though it was simply one person making a  decision but in fact we know that's not  how households make decisions most of us  in our households have many people and  although we may agree on many things we  probably don't agree on everything and  so trying to understand about the  dynamics of what happens in households  is really important because it affects  the outcomes so when women have more  power in households or less power in  households when women earn more of the  income or as we're starting to learn  when women own some of the assets it  actually affects the kinds of decisions  that households make including decisions  about if your rural households in Africa  it might affect the  decisions about what kind of crops to  grow how much of them to sell but also  decisions about sending children to  school health care and various other  kinds of things I know you've done a lot  of work as you mentioned in Liberia and  also Uganda how is land acquisition and  untenured changing in these countries so  that the everywhere in the world right  now patterns of land our land ownership  are changing in part because there's  becoming more and more pressure on land  the population is growing there's more  pressure for foreign companies are  coming in wanting to buy land in order  to grow more food to feed cities and so  the traditional patterns of land  ownership which made may not even be  ownership in the sense that we  understand it may simply be that  particular households have a right to  farm a particular plot of land but many  of them don't have any kind of documents  for ownership Wow  that's worked traditionally fairly well  there were local systems for how to  allocate how to allocate land how to  resolve conflicts and there were  traditional systems for how women had  access to land in order to be able to  farm but as both as some of the social  structures have broken down particularly  in Uganda one of the things that's  changed is an increase in hiv/aids so  you at least hear lots of stories that  when what a man dies of hiv/aids that  often his widow  will lose access to the land that she  had been farming she doesn't herself  have claim to it and other people from  the community maybe from his family who  don't have enough land will come in and  try to claim that purty piece of land so  to the extent that the system's had been  working before now that there's much  more pressure on land the systems are  starting to break down and so in  particular it's really important to  start to think about how women have  access to land and how to ensure that  particularly when their households  dissolve when their husband dies or if  he leaves how to make sure that they  still are able  to have land to support themselves and  their children  okay how do women typically own the land  or is it primarily men so it will depend  on what you mean by own so in the work  we did in Uganda over half of the  households when you actually ask them  who owns the land they will tell you  that it's the husband and wife owned it  jointly so we own the land and that  tends to be true both when we asked  husbands and when we asked wives so say  we own the land but when you actually  say well what does it mean that you own  the land whose name is on an ownership  document most of its not titled but they  may have some kind of a document that  shows who the owner is it's much much  more common for that to be in the man's  name so that it works women have access  to land they claim to be the owners but  then if anything's challenging their  rights and their husband isn't there to  support them in it they may lose lose  access to it okay and then what happens  they're kicked off the land and someone  comes I mean it seems it seems pretty  nebulous right so they may know right  they may not have access to it anymore  they may be told they can't farm that  particular piece of land somebody else  comes and farms it okay so so overall  let's talk about the gender implications  of the changes so women it sounds like  from what you're saying are becoming  worse off than they had been previously  with these changes happening or is that  incorrect they may be worse off there  could be cases where they're becoming  better off too if they have the ability  to buy land and gain a title for it then  they may be better off so there's  certainly a small group of women both in  Uganda and Liberia who own land who own  tidal land and who have really strong  claims to the land and then there's  other women but also other small holder  farmers whose access to land is being is  being threatened okay  I'm curious to know how you do your  methodology given given the topic of  households dynamic  do you actually go in and live with  anyone for a time or is it basically  surveying different families on how they  make their decisions how exactly do you  do your research so most of the work  I've been doing is survey data so we've  been collecting household survey data  interviewing multiple people within  households so where many households  simply interview the household head  we've been interviewing both the  household head whether it's a man or a  woman and other adults in the household  so in Uganda we interviewed about 350  households in three different districts  asking them questions about ownership  about documents about their rights over  the land do you have the right to sell  it can you make the decision on your own  or do you have to consult with somebody  else do you have the right to bequeath  it can you rent it out who makes the  decisions about what's get what gets  planted on the land who keeps the  revenue if you sell the the crops so a  number of different kinds of questions  to try to understand what does ownership  mean right  what is that bundle of Rights that we  see that we think of in the West as  being ownership if we owned something it  means we can sell it we can do whatever  we want with it but that's not  necessarily the case in other places  that bundle of rights may be divided up  you may have the right to farm it  without actually having the right to  sell it and does it vary within the  country for instance say within liberia  does it does it vary from a place to  place on how land ownership is is done  so both in Liberia and in Uganda there's  different forms of land ownership so  both of them have customary land tenure  systems where it's the traditional  system and that would actually what that  actually means varies across different  areas within the country and then  there's a formal legal statutory system  where some of the land has title deeds  so both of those systems occur at the  same time within one country okay are  there any track  with the changing taking place in an  acquisition and tenure I think the the  big trend is simply that there's more  and more pressure on land more people  more people wanting land and so as land  becomes more scarce than the people  without power within the household  community village country all are the  ones who are likely to lose access and  unlike this country I imagine I mean and  when I say this country I mean the  United States most of the farmland is is  controlled by big corporations there's  very few small farmers anymore in Uganda  and Liberia I would imagine and I could  be wrong in this but it seems like it's  mostly small farmers is that the case or  are there very I mean you had mentioned  there are outside companies coming in  and buying up land so how is that  changing are there up still a lot of you  know single households farmers or our  corporations taking more and more of the  land still the majority is done by small  holder farmers and still a majority of  people in both of those countries earn  their living by being small holder  farmers both countries have some kind of  large plantation agriculture and that's  growing but it's still the case that the  majority of farmers are small holder  farmers in doing your research are there  is there any one thing that particularly  surprised you I think we were surprised  in Uganda because when we went and so we  just I've been doing surveys but before  doing the surveys you have to go and do  a lot of qualitative work do a lot of  focus groups try to figure out how to  ask the right kinds of questions and  over and over  we heard statements like how can women  own property  how can property owned property and so  interests the women are considered  property people who were implying that  women were property and so how could  they be owners of property and so given  that we had heard that and that that's  part of the rhetoric about land and prop  in Uganda we were surprised that in over  50% of the households the husbands and  wives reported that they owned it  jointly which says that even though that  rhetoric is there in a lot of households  the households actually function pretty  well and the husband and wife work  together as a team to produce to produce  crops to raise a family to do all the  things that households do that to me  suggests that when we're thinking about  policies we need to think about how to  build on those households how to build  on the pieces that work so rather than  thinking about just going in and  necessarily titling land in the names of  lots of individual women how do we  ensure that in those households where  women have some claims that those claims  are strengthened okay and that if their  husbands dies the women retain the  rights to to farm that land do you think  you know moving forward 10 years or so  to your point of people saying well  women are property but yet within the  household that's not necessarily how  things work do you think moving forward  policy will be put in place where women  are thought of more as equal to men so  Uganda actually their laws are pretty  gender equal okay so they've got a  pretty good Constitution that supports  women's rights and then it's in practice  and in people's attitudes that that it  may or may not be be taking place so I  think some of those kinds of things are  changing there's certainly legislation  being discussed that would change some  of it I've been doing some other  comparative work so doing much much  larger surveys in three other countries  in India in Ecuador and in Ghana okay  where we've done similar kinds of things  asking about land and asking about all  the assets that people own and who owns  them down to the point of who owns the  cellphone and who owns the television is  that owned jointly does one of you own  it and one of the things that we find  from doing the comparative work is the  the rules about marriage and inheritance  play a big part in terms of what women  own and what women have access to so  that in places where women when you're  married you jointly owned all of the  marital property that gives women much  more ownership of assets then places  where you still own assets individually  regardless of whether you're married so  in many places including in Uganda  there's no sense of there being marital  joint property in terms of the law  whatever he has it's his and whatever  she has is hers women clearly benefit  when whatever they whatever is acquired  during marriage jointly belongs to both  of them so as long as women are still  paid less than men and take more time  out of the workforce which is true I  think everywhere then women are going to  benefit by having laws that guarantee  that half of what the marital property  is belongs to them okay very good thank  you so much for being here today I'm  sharing some of your work thank you very  much for more information about  Professor das and her research please  visit our website at Yale GDU backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

